{A recorded Yunker Exchange QGD. It reaches the same position after 4...exd5 as Reichfeld’s matched account, by a different move order. His later ...Be7/...Be6 sequence is not covered by this source.} 1. d4 {Put a pawn in the center. From d4 it controls c5 and e5; your pieces will support this space.} Nf6 2. c4 {Challenge Black’s d5 square with the c-pawn. This is the central question behind the Queen’s Gambit.} e6 3. Nc3 {Develop the queenside knight toward the center. On c3 it controls d5 and e4.} d5 4. cxd5 {Exchange on d5. After …exd5, the central pawn structure is fixed and the c-file is half-open for White.} exd5 5. Bg5 {Pin the f6-knight to the queen on d8. This makes Black’s central defender less flexible.} c6 6. Qc2 {Place the queen on c2. It supports e4 and helps coordinate a bishop on d3.} Be7 7. e3 {Support d4 and open the f1-bishop’s diagonal. The c1-bishop has already escaped the pawn chain.} O-O 8. Bd3 {Develop the bishop to d3, facing h7. Complete development before launching a pawn attack.} h6 9. Bh4 Nbd7 10. Nf3 Re8 11. Bg3 b6 12. O-O Bb7 13. Rfd1 Nf8 14. Rac1 Bd6 15. Bh4 Rc8 16. a4 N8d7 17. Bf5 Rc7 18. Bg3 Bxg3 19. hxg3 a6 20. b4 Qe7 21. Qb2 c5 22. bxc5 bxc5 23. dxc5 Nxc5 24. Nd4 Nfe4 25. Nxe4 dxe4 26. Qb6 Bc8 27. Nc6 Rxc6 28. Qxc6 Bxf5 29. Qxc5 Qf6 30. Rd6 Qb2 31. Rb6 Qd2 32. Qc3 Qe2 33. Re1 Qa2 34. Rxa6 h5 35. Ra5 Bg6 36. Rf1 Qe6 37. Rc5 h4 38. gxh4 Qg4 39. Qc4 Kh7 40. Rg5 Qxh4 41. Rg3 Kg8 42. Rxg6 *

