{A separate verified Yunker Nge2 model. Learn why f3/e4 stays available, but do not combine its move order blindly with the other QGD game.} 1. d4 {Put a pawn in the center. From d4 it controls c5 and e5; your pieces will support this space.} d5 2. c4 {Challenge Black’s d5 square with the c-pawn. This is the central question behind the Queen’s Gambit.} e6 3. Nc3 {Develop the queenside knight toward the center. On c3 it controls d5 and e4.} Nf6 4. cxd5 {Exchange the c-pawn for Black’s d-pawn and recognize the same pawn structure.} exd5 5. Bg5 {Develop the bishop actively before playing e3.} Be6 6. e3 {Open the f1-bishop’s diagonal and reinforce d4.} Nbd7 7. Bd3 {Aim the bishop toward h7 while clearing f1 for castling.} c6 8. Nge2 {Use e2 for this knight. Unlike Nf3, it leaves the f-pawn free for a possible f3–e4 plan.} h6 9. Bh4 g5 10. Bg3 Nh5 11. Qc2 Nxg3 12. Nxg3 h5 13. Nge2 Qa5 14. h4 g4 15. Bf5 O-O-O 16. Nf4 Bxf5 17. Qxf5 g3 18. O-O gxf2+ 19. Rxf2 Be7 20. Rc2 Bxh4 21. b4 Qxb4 22. Nfxd5 Qd6 23. Nb5 Qg3 24. Nxa7+ Kb8 25. Nxc6+ bxc6 26. Rb1+ Ka7 27. Qd3 Qd6 28. Rc3 Be7 29. Nxe7 Nb6 30. Ra3+ Qxa3 31. Qxa3+ Kb7 32. Qc5 Rd7 33. Qxc6+ Ka7 34. Qxb6+ Ka8 35. Qa6+ Ra7 36. Qc6+ Rb7 37. Qxb7# *

